    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-02",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Closed contours with elevations increasing inward commonly indicate a hill, with a summit or spot height inside. A depression needs the relevant signs or decreasing elevations rather than being assumed from any closed shape. The training relief panel uses surrounding contours at 1,510,1,520 and 1,530 metres with a 1,540-metre spot height inside. Its labelled rise supports a hill interpretation.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "bb57ebe9e2e3ee97425e202e238a6d30fb6e7623d0fe7d2e699342b9b93e55e7"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-03",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "In a valley the contour lines form V or U shapes that point upstream, towards higher contour values, and a river often runs along the line of the Vs. A spur is the opposite: a tongue of high land whose V-shaped contours point downhill, towards lower contour values. To tell them apart, read the heights on the contours on either side of the V, and check whether a river symbol follows it.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-R1"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-R1": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "d655c616c8a5b51071c9d1cca8a9ab40a34289fc369c96861c8e2d6b60f401de",
      "prior_hash": "273f794d9f187cf559b55de69603051d179acd7dea74fc553eb7773bb27dc564",
      "repair_note": "Claude cross-review repairs: 6137-4.2-001, 6137-4.2-002, 6137-4.2-003, 6137-4.2-004, 6137-4.2-005, 6137-4.2-006"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-04",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Relief describes shape and elevation; drainage describes watercourses and flow. A river line should not be substituted for a slope description. Conversely, contour spacing does not establish a permanent water supply. The distinction matters when explaining farming, settlement or transport: slope can affect construction or runoff, while water access depends on the source and conditions.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "3539ebae9b87ad50e948153e6c7ee6c729eee117ef7c4e2df033e17fc0b8d062"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-05",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Symbols can add evidence about cliffs, marshy land, water bodies, vegetation or human use. Interpret the supplied key instead of assigning a feature from a familiar colour. A cliff symbol indicates a landform representation, while the map scale may not give every local detail. A marsh symbol supports wet-ground interpretation at the map's date, not an unlimited current water source or a complete account of seasonal variation.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "ae0d36ea4892e01bea43f975745aa1d29c5456a9ab89b8c9fed60b0bb7860543"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-06",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A supplied cross-section can be read to describe relative height, slope or the position of a valley. The task here is interpretation rather than constructing a new cross-section. Check its horizontal and vertical scales before comparing shape; vertical exaggeration can make a gentle profile look dramatic. A visual angle on an exaggerated diagram is not the actual ground slope angle.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "2353e4d7964c905ba037ad7f4df0699a51c9772cf1c44a4ebea8b02166e5e9bd"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-07",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A slope comparison should use the same contour interval. If one map uses ten-metre intervals and another twenty, equal paper spacing represents different vertical change. Likewise, resizing one panel affects visual spacing. Use labels and scale, not appearance alone. These checks prevent a neat-looking contour pattern from being read as an exact height or gradient without the necessary measurements.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "e33e1eb6560030c3d48d9b1c4e1c239ead4b438cc7bbbd4667531f4be74f1f24"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-08",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A sound landform answer identifies the feature and cites the pattern establishing it. For a hill, inward-rising closed contours and the spot height are relevant. For a valley, contour bends and the channel/height sequence matter. A nearby settlement's name or imagined tourist activity does not replace that physical evidence. Later human-activity interpretations can then use the actual relief rather than inventing it.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "92b840709e10ba3fa733ac3c6860ad7a6f741c604a9ac39656145f710ffb5a5e"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-09",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Contour spacing can vary along a slope. Wide spacing near the foot and closer spacing higher up indicates a change in steepness under equal intervals. A spot-height difference divided by the whole horizontal run gives an average that can conceal this variation. Both descriptions can be correct. The contours describe the shape in more detail, while the ratio summarises the chosen endpoints.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "4b5d4e577e70482c4d94bc0011c90de43722686fff7352878a4171934d6fe3fb"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Other landforms can be read from contours. A plateau is a large flat-topped upland, shown by widely spaced contours or none on top and closely spaced contours around its edges. An escarpment is a long steep slope, shown by a band of closely spaced contours, often at the edge of a plateau. A ridge is a long narrow stretch of high ground; a saddle is a lower dip between two hills; a gorge is a deep valley with very close contours on both sides; and an inselberg is an isolated steep-sided hill rising from a plain.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-R1"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-R1": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "160d9763c8825415de13844a1ff5c72de8cc5d58e3d2aec59e5cdaf2fed73a8c",
      "prior_hash": "9eb0588f43c388ee81349181408a39b1860a4839899ea902ae00e47f1e1baa57",
      "repair_note": "Claude cross-review repairs: 6137-4.2-001, 6137-4.2-002, 6137-4.2-003, 6137-4.2-004, 6137-4.2-005, 6137-4.2-006"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-11",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A supplied profile also needs matching endpoints. If it represents A–B, a valley beyond that line cannot be inferred from its shape. A vertical scale in metres and horizontal scale in kilometres can create substantial exaggeration; use numerical changes rather than the visual angle alone. The learning target is to interpret the supplied profile and map evidence, not to construct an unrequested section.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2"
      ],
      "context_tags": [
        "Geography",
        "original teaching"
      ],
      "assessment_objectives": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C"
      ],
      "claims_seen": {
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1": 1,
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-2": 1
      },
      "authored_hash": "1e4dc89682221427154a0398b19313f8e3bc3e604b648fd0f40a1e86ba491012"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.04-12",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "To find the height of a point, use the contour lines. A point that lies on a contour line has that line's height, even if a spot height printed nearby shows a slightly different value; a point between two contours lies between their values. Count the interval carefully: if contours are drawn every 20 m and a point lies on the third line above the 1 400 m contour, its height is 1 460 m.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.04-02-1",
